                    round_name_r4:          `Recover`,

                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose: map the change landscape`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `Adoption has stalled at <strong>34%</strong> against an 80% target; resistance has climbed to <strong>51/100</strong> and engagement is down 14 points. The rollout repeated Maasland’s historic pattern — a launch town-hall, one training session, then silence. Using the Change Canvas, separate the three root-cause gaps: the <em>“why”</em> (no compelling case for change), the <em>“ability”</em> (one-off training), and the <em>“reinforcement”</em> gap (managers not modelling the behaviour). Read the resistance index as a <strong>signal</strong>, not an obstacle, and pick the highest-leverage stakeholders to engage first.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Plan: intervention intensity, cadence & sequence`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Design the intervention portfolio under the remaining <strong>≈€790K</strong>. Choose an <em>intervention intensity</em> (light comms + e-learning, medium workshops + coaching, or heavy embedded agents + bootcamps), set a <em>communication cadence</em> (a one-off broadcast vs. a sustained two-way rhythm), and decide the <em>engagement order</em>. Van Olffen’s discovery: front-loading <strong>line-manager enablement before the frontline</strong> bends the adoption curve more than any volume of communication. Cadence, not volume, drives belief.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Decide: sequence the waves & commit the spend`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `The core round. Sequence the <strong>12 remaining teams</strong> into waves and commit budget across the nine-month window. Decide whether to <em>stabilise the six struggling live teams first</em> (rescue) or <em>push forward</em> (momentum) — two teams have already reverted, and a wider audience is watching. Change-agent and coaching capacity is finite, so not every team can get heavy support: <strong>intensity is a budget you ration</strong>. Heavy embeds faster but starves the final waves; light stretches the budget but risks the 40% plateau.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Recover: embed, reinforce & defend to the board`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Two shocks land. A <strong>vocal middle-manager coalition</strong> publicly questions the programme in a town-hall, and a <em>regulatory reporting deadline</em> pulls 20% of frontline time for a month. Protect adoption, convert the sceptical managers into advocates, and install the <strong>reinforcement mechanism</strong> that makes the behaviour stick after the change agents leave — peer champions, visible sponsor sponsorship, metrics in daily routines. Then defend the platform renewal to the board: adoption on track to 80%, resistance falling, budget intact.`,

                    r1_rootcause_label:     `What is the dominant root cause of the 34% stall?`,
                    rootcause_awareness:    `An awareness gap — people simply haven’t heard the message enough; we need more communication`,
                    rootcause_ability:      `An ability gap — one training session wasn’t enough; people can’t use the tool yet`,
                    rootcause_reinforcement:`A reinforcement gap — managers don’t model or reinforce the behaviour, so it never embeds`,
                    rootcause_resistance:   `Pure resistance — some people just don’t want to change and never will`,
                    r1_signal_label:        `How do you read the resistance index climbing from 28 to 51?`,
                    signal_obstacle:        `As an obstacle to overpower — push harder with sponsor authority`,
                    signal_diagnostic:      `As a diagnostic signal — it points to the specific gap (“no one explained why, and my manager doesn’t use it”)`,
                    signal_ignore:          `As noise — pulse scores are volatile, focus on the rollout plan instead`,
                    r1_stakeholder_label:   `Which stakeholders are highest-leverage to engage first?`,
                    stakeholder_frontline:  `The frontline staff — they are the ones who must use the tool daily`,
                    stakeholder_sponsors:   `The executive sponsors — secure more air-cover and visible authority from the top`,
                    stakeholder_managers:   `The line managers — they are the multiplier who must model and reinforce the behaviour`,
                    r1_focus_label:         `How sharply do you focus the diagnosis on the reinforcement gap vs. spreading effort across all gaps? (%)`,
                    r1_focus_readout:       `Diagnostic focus on reinforcement:`,
                    r1_pct:                 `%`,

                    r2_intensity_label:     `Baseline intervention intensity for the remaining waves.`,
                    intensity_light:        `Light touch — comms + e-learning only; cheapest, stretches the budget furthest`,
                    intensity_medium:       `Medium — add workshops + floor coaching; balanced cost and embedding`,
                    intensity_heavy:        `Heavy everywhere — embedded change agents + manager bootcamps for every team`,
                    r2_cadence_label:       `Communication cadence.`,
                    cadence_oneoff:         `One-off broadcast — a second town-hall and a fresh email campaign; raise the volume`,
                    cadence_sustained:      `Sustained two-way rhythm — weekly stand-up dialogue that surfaces resistance and answers “why” repeatedly`,
                    r2_order_label:         `Engagement order — who do you enable first?`,
                    order_frontlinefirst:   `Frontline first — train the 400 staff thoroughly, managers will catch up`,
                    order_parallel:         `Everyone in parallel — train managers and frontline at the same time`,
                    order_managersfirst:    `Managers first — enable line managers to lead the behaviour before the frontline is trained`,
                    r2_managerspend_label:  `Share of this phase’s spend you front-load into line-manager enablement (%)`,
                    r2_managerspend_readout:`Spend front-loaded into managers:`,
                    r2_pct:                 `%`,
                    r2_managerspend_hint:   `Van Olffen’s multiplier: managers who model the behaviour move adoption more than any volume of communication. Too little here repeats the 40% plateau.`,

                    r3_rescue_label:        `The six struggling live teams vs. launching new waves.`,
                    rescue_pushforward:     `Push forward — launch new waves to keep momentum; the reverted teams will recover on their own`,
                    rescue_rescuefirst:     `Stabilise first — rescue the six live teams before exposing more teams to a spreading failure`,
                    rescue_abandon:         `Abandon the two reverted teams — cut losses and focus only on fresh waves`,
                    r3_dosage_label:        `How do you ration finite change-agent and coaching capacity across the 12 remaining teams?`,
                    dosage_alllight:        `All light — give every team the same cheap support to stretch the budget`,
                    dosage_allheavy:        `All heavy — full intensity for every team to embed as fast as possible`,
                    dosage_matched:         `Match dose to readiness — heavy for the least-ready teams, medium for the rest; intensity is a budget you ration`,
                    r3_waves_label:         `How do you sequence the 12 remaining teams into waves?`,
                    waves_bigbang:          `Big-bang — all 12 at once to finish inside the window; speed over support depth`,
                    waves_overlapping:      `Overlapping waves — staggered ≈5 months/team so capacity is reused and each wave learns from the last`,
                    waves_serial:           `Strictly serial — one wave fully embedded before the next; safest but overruns the 9-month window`,
                    r3_commit_label:        `Budget you commit to this wave plan now (€K of the remaining ≈€790K)`,
                    commit_low:             `€250K — hold most in reserve; light support across the board`,
                    commit_mid:             `€500K — fund matched dosage for the early waves, keep a reserve for the final teams`,
                    commit_high:            `€780K — spend almost everything now on full support for the early waves`,

                    r4_coalition_label:     `A vocal middle-manager coalition questions the programme in the town-hall. Your response.`,
                    coalition_overrule:     `Overrule them — invoke sponsor authority and tell them the decision is made`,
                    coalition_convert:      `Convert them — listen to what they fear, address it, and turn the loudest sceptic into a credible champion`,
                    coalition_ignore:       `Ignore them — they’ll fall in line once adoption rises elsewhere`,
                    r4_regulatory_label:    `A regulatory reporting deadline pulls 20% of frontline time for a month. What do you do?`,
                    regulatory_pushboth:    `Push both at full pace — refuse to slow the rollout; people will cope`,
                    regulatory_protect:     `Protect the core — defer non-essential change activity, keep the daily reinforcement rhythm alive`,
                    regulatory_pause:       `Pause the rollout entirely for the month — restart from scratch afterwards`,
                    r4_mechanism_label:     `The reinforcement mechanism that makes the behaviour stick after the change agents leave.`,
                    mechanism_celebrate:    `Declare success at each go-live and celebrate — momentum will carry it`,
                    mechanism_audit:        `A compliance audit — track who isn’t using the tool and escalate to their manager`,
                    mechanism_embed:        `Peer champions + visible sponsor sponsorship + success metrics built into daily team routines`,
                    r4_board_label:         `Your board recommendation framing.`,
                    board_spin:             `Lead with the best number — adoption spiked at go-live; downplay the decay`,
                    board_defer:            `Ask for more time and budget — we need another nine months and another €0.5M`,
                    board_evidence:         `Lead with the evidence — adoption on track to 80%, resistance falling, budget intact, renewal justified`,
                    r4_conviction_label:    `Conviction: how firmly do you hold that reinforcement and manager modelling, not launch energy, drive lasting adoption? (%)`,

                    warn_focus_range:       `Diagnostic focus must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_managerspend_range:`Manager-enablement share must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_commit_value:      `The committed budget cannot be negative.`,
                    warn_conviction_range:  `Conviction must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,

                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_reinforcement: `<strong>That’s the right diagnosis.</strong> The dominant theme in the pulse survey is “no one explained why, and my manager doesn’t use it either.” That is a <em>reinforcement</em> gap, not an awareness gap — and you can’t fix it by communicating harder. The question is what changes about how managers behave on Monday.`,
                    coach_r1_ability:       `<strong>Partly right — there is an ability gap.</strong> One training session wasn’t enough. But the deeper signal is that managers aren’t modelling the behaviour, so even well-trained staff revert. Ability without reinforcement still decays to the 40% plateau.`,
                    coach_r1_awareness:     `<strong>“People just haven’t heard it enough” is the broadcast bias.</strong> You doubled the emails and ran another town-hall — and resistance went UP, from 51 to 56. People told you the problem wasn’t that they hadn’t heard; it’s that their manager still uses the spreadsheet. More broadcast doesn’t fix a reinforcement gap.`,
                    coach_r1_resistance:    `<strong>“Some people just won’t change” writes off the signal.</strong> Resistance carries information about unaddressed fears and unmet needs. Treat it as a character flaw and you’ll push harder on exactly the wrong lever — and watch attrition climb.`,
                    coach_r1_obstacle:      `<strong>Resistance is a signal, not an enemy to overpower.</strong> Invoke sponsor authority over the pulse and the resistant managers go quiet in the town-hall and louder in the corridor. Ask what they’re actually afraid of before you reach for power.`,
                    coach_r1_ignore:        `<strong>Calling the resistance score “noise” throws away your best diagnostic.</strong> The index climbed from 28 to 51 for a reason — it’s telling you precisely where the case for change or the reinforcement is failing. Read it; don’t mute it.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named the reinforcement gap, read resistance as a signal, and chose the line managers — the multiplier — to engage first. That’s the Change Canvas working as intended: design and monitor the change, then intervene on the specific gap.`,
                    coach_r2_managersfirst: `<strong>Managers first — the highest-leverage sequencing decision in the programme.</strong> Enable the line managers to lead the behaviour before the frontline is trained, and on day two the manager reinforces instead of saying “just get the cases out however you can.” Adoption climbs and holds.`,
                    coach_r2_frontlinefirst:`<strong>You trained 400 frontline staff beautifully — and skipped the multiplier.</strong> Their managers were never enabled, so on day two adoption collapses back to the old 40% plateau. In van Olffen’s logic the line manager is the multiplier; train the frontline first and you’re filling a leaking bucket.`,
                    coach_r2_broadcast:     `<strong>A one-off broadcast is exactly what failed the first time.</strong> A launch announcement is a broadcast; embedding needs a sustained, two-way rhythm that surfaces resistance early and answers “why” repeatedly. Cadence, not volume, drives belief.`,
                    coach_r2_heavy:         `<strong>Heavy everywhere looks decisive and is the dosage trap.</strong> Embedded agents and bootcamps for every team embed fast — until you’ve burned the budget by wave eight and the final teams revert. Intensity is a budget you ration. Which teams genuinely need heavy, and which only need medium?`,
                    coach_r2_light:         `<strong>Light touch plus a one-off broadcast is the historic plateau in a new wrapper.</strong> Comms and e-learning alone are what plateaued adoption at 40% before. You’re repeating the pattern and expecting a different number.`,
                    coach_r3_rescue:        `<strong>Stabilise the wound before you cut again.</strong> Rescuing the six live teams — two of them reverted — stops a spreading failure and gives the watching teams a visible success to follow. A reverted team in plain sight costs more than a slower schedule.`,
                    coach_r3_momentum:      `<strong>You launched new waves while two of the original six have reverted.</strong> Now you have a spreading failure and a widening audience watching it fail. Momentum doesn’t outrun reversion — it advertises it. Did you stabilise the wound before cutting again?`,
                    coach_r3_abandon:       `<strong>Abandoning the reverted teams is the loudest possible failure signal.</strong> The twelve teams still to come are watching what happens to people who struggled. Write the strugglers off and you’ve told everyone what reverting earns them — abandonment.`,
                    coach_r3_allheavy:      `<strong>All-heavy across twelve teams is the budget exhaustion you can already see coming.</strong> You’ll have spent the lot by wave eight and have nothing left for the final four, which revert. Match dose to readiness instead of treating every team identically.`,
                    coach_r3_alllight:      `<strong>All-light stretches the budget straight back to the 40% plateau.</strong> A uniform cheap dose ignores that the least-ready teams need real support. Matching intensity to readiness is what makes a fixed budget go further — not spreading it thin.`,
                    coach_r3_bigbang:       `<strong>A big-bang across all 12 teams trades support depth for speed.</strong> Finishing inside the window means nothing if the finite change agents are spread so thin that nothing embeds. Overlapping waves reuse capacity and let each wave learn from the last.`,
                    coach_r3_serial:        `<strong>Strictly serial is the safest per team and overruns the window.</strong> One wave fully embedded before the next pushes the last teams past the month-10 renewal. The waves have to overlap — that’s what the ≈5-months/team target assumes.`,
                    coach_r3_overspent:     `<strong>You’ve committed almost the whole budget to the early waves.</strong> With under €60K left, the final teams get nothing and revert — the uniform-intensity trap. A change budget is rationed across all the teams, not front-loaded onto the first ones.`,
                    coach_r4_convert:       `<strong>Converting the coalition is resistance-as-information in action.</strong> You listened to what the managers actually feared, addressed it, and turned the loudest sceptic into your most credible champion. A manager who was against it and changed their mind persuades peers far better than any sponsor email.`,
                    coach_r4_overrule:      `<strong>You tried to overrule the coalition with sponsor authority.</strong> They went quiet in the town-hall and louder in the corridor — engagement dropped another eight points. Resistance is a signal, not an enemy. What are these managers actually afraid of?`,
                    coach_r4_coalignore:    `<strong>Ignoring the coalition lets the corridor win.</strong> Vocal sceptical managers who feel unheard become the reference group their teams trust more than your dashboard. Unaddressed, they don’t fall in line — they set the line.`,
                    coach_r4_pushboth:      `<strong>Pushing rollout and regulatory work at full pace overloads the frontline.</strong> With 20% of their time gone to the deadline, demanding full change activity too tells people the change isn’t real work. Protect the core reinforcement rhythm and defer the rest.`,
                    coach_r4_pause:         `<strong>Pausing entirely lets the behaviour decay.</strong> A month of silence is exactly the gap in which trained behaviour slides back to the spreadsheet. Protect the daily rhythm at a lower intensity rather than going dark and restarting cold.`,
                    coach_r4_embed:         `<strong>That’s an embedding system, not a launch event.</strong> Peer champions, visible sponsor sponsorship and success metrics in daily routines keep the behaviour alive after the change agents leave — which is exactly what every previous Maasland rollout lacked when it decayed to 45% a month after go-live.`,
                    coach_r4_audit:         `<strong>A compliance audit reinforces fear, not behaviour.</strong> Tracking who isn’t using the tool and escalating to managers makes adoption a surveillance exercise — resistance climbs and people comply on the dashboard while reverting in practice. Reinforcement is built with champions and routines, not policing.`,
                    coach_r4_celebrate:     `<strong>Declaring success at go-live is the historic failure.</strong> Adoption hit 70% the week after each previous launch — and decayed to 45% a month later. Training is an event; embedding is a system. What keeps the behaviour alive after the change agents leave?`,
                    coach_r4_spin:          `<strong>Leading with the best number is how a CFO stops trusting you.</strong> The board can see the decay curve. Point at the go-live spike and downplay the slide, and they’ll assume the rest is worse than it is — and pull the licence.`,
                    coach_r4_defer:         `<strong>Asking for another nine months and €0.5M concedes the programme failed.</strong> The renewal decision is month 10 either way. A request for more time without evidence of embedding reads as “it didn’t work” — and the CFO writes off the €1.2M.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Named the reinforcement gap; read resistance as signal; managers first.`,
                    sum_r1_weak:            `Mis-diagnosed the gap or chose the wrong first stakeholder.`,
                    sum_r2_good:            `Managers-first, sustained cadence, dose matched to budget.`,
                    sum_r2_heavy:           `Heavy everywhere — the dosage trap.`,
                    sum_r2_frontline:       `Trained the frontline before the managers — skipped the multiplier.`,
                    sum_r2_broadcast:       `Relied on a one-off broadcast — the broadcast bias.`,
                    sum_r2_weak:            `Portfolio under-tuned on cadence or sequence.`,
                    sum_r3_good:            `Rescued the live teams; matched dosage; overlapping waves.`,
                    sum_r3_momentum:        `Pushed forward over a spreading failure.`,
                    sum_r3_allheavy:        `All-heavy — heading for budget exhaustion.`,
                    sum_r3_alllight:        `All-light — stretched the budget back to the plateau.`,
                    sum_r3_weak:            `Wave plan partially sequenced.`,
                    sum_r4_good:            `Converted the coalition; protected the core; built an embedding system.`,
                    sum_r4_weak:            `Overpowered resistance, declared success, or spun the board.`,

                    insight_embedded:       `Embedded and renewed: you diagnosed the reinforcement gap, put line managers first, matched intervention dose to readiness, rescued the early failures, and built a real embedding system. Adoption is on track to 80%, resistance has fallen, the budget held, and the platform renewal is justified — the behaviour will outlast the change agents.`,
                    insight_decay:          `Launch-as-success: you treated go-live energy as embedding and skipped the reinforcement mechanism. Adoption spiked then decayed back toward the historic 40% plateau, exactly like every previous Maasland rollout. Training is an event; embedding is a system you never built.`,
                    insight_nomultiplier:   `Frontline-before-managers: you trained the staff but never enabled the line managers who must model the behaviour. On day two the managers didn’t reinforce it and adoption collapsed to the plateau — you skipped van Olffen’s multiplier.`,
                    insight_broadcast:      `Broadcast bias: you answered a reinforcement gap with more communication volume. The emails and town-halls raised the noise, not the adoption, and resistance climbed — cadence and modelling, not message volume, drive belief.`,
                    insight_overspent:      `Budget exhausted: you lavished heavy intensity on the early waves and ran the change budget dry before the final teams, which reverted. Intensity is a rationed resource — uniform heavy support starves the teams you reach last.`,
                    insight_momentum:       `Spreading failure: you pushed new waves forward while the early teams were visibly reverting, advertising the failure to a widening audience. Stabilising the wound before cutting again would have given the watching teams a success to follow.`,
                    insight_mixed:          `A mixed rollout: some of the change design landed, but diagnosis, sequencing, dosage and reinforcement didn’t fully reinforce each other — enough gaps for adoption to wobble and for the board to doubt the renewal.`,
                    insight_cancelled:      `Adoption finished below the 60% network-wide cancel threshold: on this trajectory the CFO cancels the platform at the month-10 renewal, the €1.2M is written off, and the transformation is declared a failure.`,
                    insight_resistance_high:`Resistance never came down: the index stayed high because the specific gap — the missing “why” or the missing reinforcement — was never addressed, only pushed against. Resistance is a signal; it kept telling you what you didn’t fix.`,
                    insight_slow_embed:     `Adoption reached target but time-to-embed stayed slow: the behaviour is adopted but not yet self-sustaining, so it remains vulnerable to the next competing priority. Shorter time-to-embed comes from reinforcement built into daily routines.`,
                    insight_multiplier_used:`The line-manager multiplier did its work: enabling managers to model and reinforce the behaviour before the frontline is what bent the adoption curve and held it against the shocks.`,
                    insight_nobudget:       `You finished with almost no budget left: little reserve remained for reinforcement after the change agents leave, leaving the embedding dependent on goodwill rather than a funded mechanism.`,

                    hint_cancel:            `Adoption is below the 60% cancel threshold — on this path the CFO pulls the licence at month 10. Fix the reinforcement gap and enable managers fast before the next round.`,
                    hint_managers:          `You trained the frontline but never enabled their managers — adoption is sliding to the plateau. Put line managers first; they are the multiplier who must model the behaviour.`,
                    hint_broadcast:         `You’re answering a reinforcement gap with more communication. Resistance is climbing because the volume isn’t the problem — switch to a sustained two-way cadence and change what managers do.`,
                    hint_budget:            `Heavy intensity everywhere is draining the budget — the final waves will get nothing and revert. Match dose to readiness and keep a reserve for the last teams.`,
                    hint_rescue:            `You pushed forward over a spreading failure. Stabilise the reverted live teams before exposing more teams; a visible reversion costs more than a slower schedule.`,
                    hint_reinforce:         `You leaned on launch energy without a reinforcement mechanism — the behaviour will decay to 40% like every previous rollout. Build peer champions, sponsor visibility and metrics into daily routines.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — adoption above target and resistance low. Land it: convert the manager coalition and lock in an embedding system before you face the board.`,

